\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fItree\fP The tree holding the node\&. Must not be NULL\&. 
.br
\fInode\fP The node to invalidate\&. 
.RE
.PP
\fBReturns\fP
.RS 4
SCHULTZ_OK, or SCHULTZ_ERR_INVALID_HANDLE\&. 
.RE
.PP

.SS "int32_t schultz_node_is_dirty (const \fBschultz_tree\fP * tree, \fBschultz_handle\fP node)"

.PP
Reports whether a node is itself marked dirty\&. 
.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fItree\fP The tree holding the node\&. NULL yields 0\&. 
.br
\fInode\fP The node to query\&. 
.RE
.PP
\fBReturns\fP
.RS 4
1 when the node's own pixels need repainting, 0 otherwise\&. 
.RE
.PP

.SS "int32_t schultz_node_subtree_dirty (const \fBschultz_tree\fP * tree, \fBschultz_handle\fP node)"

.PP
Reports whether a node or anything beneath it is dirty\&. A paint walk tests this to decide whether to descend, which is what lets a clean subtree cost nothing\&.
.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fItree\fP The tree holding the node\&. NULL yields 0\&. 
.br
\fInode\fP The node to query\&. 
.RE
.PP
\fBReturns\fP
.RS 4
1 when the subtree contains a dirty node, 0 otherwise\&. 
.RE
.PP

.SS "int32_t schultz_tree_is_dirty (const \fBschultz_tree\fP * tree)"

.PP
Reports whether anything in the tree needs repainting\&. 
.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fItree\fP The tree to query\&. NULL yields 0\&. 
.RE
.PP
\fBReturns\fP
.RS 4
1 when a repaint is needed, 0 when the frame can be skipped\&. 
.RE
.PP

.SS "uint32_t schultz_tree_dirty_count (const \fBschultz_tree\fP * tree)"

.PP
How many separate areas need repainting\&. The repainting a tree asks for is a few rectangles rather than one, because one means two small changes at opposite corners repaint everything between them\&. docs/repainting\&.md explains it and gives the measurements\&.
.PP
Zero means nothing changed\&.
.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fItree\fP The tree to query\&. NULL yields zero\&. 
.RE
.PP
\fBReturns\fP
.RS 4
How many, at most SCHULTZ_TREE_DIRTY_PARTS\&. 
.RE
.PP

.SS "int32_t schultz_tree_dirty_at (const \fBschultz_tree\fP * tree, uint32_t index, \fBschultz_rect\fP * out_region)"

.PP
Returns one of the areas that need repainting\&. They do not overlap in any way worth relying on: a caller that paints each in turn may paint a pixel twice, which costs a little and is never wrong\&.
.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fItree\fP The tree to query\&. Must not be NULL\&. 
.br
\fIindex\fP From zero to schultz_tree_dirty_count minus one\&. 
.br
\fIout_region\fP Receives it\&. Must not be NULL\&. 
.RE
.PP
\fBReturns\fP
.RS 4
SCHULTZ_OK, SCHULTZ_ERR_INVALID_ARGUMENT, or SCHULTZ_ERR_EXHAUSTED when there is no such area\&. 
.RE
.PP

.SS "int32_t schultz_tree_dirty_region (const \fBschultz_tree\fP * tree, \fBschultz_rect\fP * out_region)"

.PP
Returns the union of every dirty region, in window coordinates\&. This is the rectangle the rasterizer should be restricted to and the presentation layer should upload\&.
.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fItree\fP The tree to query\&. Must not be NULL\&. 
.br
\fIout_region\fP Receives the union, clipped to the viewport\&. Must not be NULL\&. An empty rectangle means nothing needs repainting\&. 
.RE
.PP
\fBReturns\fP
.RS 4
SCHULTZ_OK, or SCHULTZ_ERR_INVALID_ARGUMENT when either pointer is NULL\&. 
.RE
.PP

.SS "void schultz_tree_clear_dirty (\fBschultz_tree\fP * tree)"

.PP
Clears every dirty mark in the tree\&. Call once a frame has been painted and presented\&. Clearing before painting loses the repaint\&.
.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fItree\fP The tree to clear\&. NULL is accepted and does nothing\&. 
.RE
.PP

.SS "uint32_t schultz_tree_node_count (const \fBschultz_tree\fP * tree)"

.PP
Counts live nodes, including the root\&. 
.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fItree\fP The tree to query\&. NULL yields 0\&. 
.RE
.PP
\fBReturns\fP
.RS 4
The number of nodes\&. 
.RE
.PP

.SS "int32_t schultz_style_register (\fBschultz_tree\fP * tree, const \fBschultz_patch\fP * patch, \fBschultz_handle\fP * out_style)"

.PP
Registers a reusable style and returns a handle for it\&. The patch is copied, so the caller's may be released afterwards\&. A style is immutable once registered: to change one, register another\&. Styles are reference counted and released when the last node using one drops it, or when the tree is destroyed\&.
.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fItree\fP The tree to register with\&. Must not be NULL\&. 
.br
\fIpatch\fP The properties this style sets\&. Must not be NULL\&. 
.br
\fIout_style\fP Receives the style handle\&. Must not be NULL\&. 
.RE
.PP
\fBReturns\fP
.RS 4
SCHULTZ_OK, SCHULTZ_ERR_INVALID_ARGUMENT for a NULL pointer, or SCHULTZ_ERR_OUT_OF_MEMORY\&. 
.RE
.PP

.SS "int32_t schultz_style_register_states (\fBschultz_tree\fP * tree, const \fBschultz_patch\fP * patch, const \fBschultz_patch\fP * states, \fBschultz_handle\fP * out_style)"

.PP
Registers a reusable style that also says how it looks in each interaction state\&. The same as schultz_style_register, plus the hovered, pressed and disabled looks that go with the plain one\&. Those belong to the style rather than to the nodes wearing it, so a rule written once is shared by every node that takes the style, and dropping the style with schultz_node_remove_style takes its state rules away too\&. Set them on the node with schultz_node_set_state_property and neither is true: the rule is copied per node, and removing the style leaves it behind\&.
.PP
Where it lands in the cascade: above every layer that has no state, so a hover colour beats the plain one whether that came from a style or from the node; below the node's own state patches, so naming a state on one node still wins, the way an inline property beats a style\&.
.PP
Every patch is copied, so the caller's may be released afterwards\&.
.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fItree\fP The tree to register with\&. Must not be NULL\&. 
.br
\fIpatch\fP The properties this style sets whatever state a node is in\&. Must not be NULL\&. 
.br
\fIstates\fP SCHULTZ_STYLE_STATE_COUNT patches, indexed by the SCHULTZ_STYLE_STATE_* values, or NULL for a style with no state rules\&. An empty patch means that state adds nothing\&. 
.br
\fIout_style\fP Receives the style handle\&. Must not be NULL\&. 
.RE
.PP
\fBReturns\fP
.RS 4
SCHULTZ_OK, SCHULTZ_ERR_INVALID_ARGUMENT for a NULL pointer, or SCHULTZ_ERR_OUT_OF_MEMORY\&. 
.RE
.PP

.SS "int32_t schultz_node_add_style (\fBschultz_tree\fP * tree, \fBschultz_handle\fP node, \fBschultz_handle\fP style)"

.PP
Adds a style to a node's ordered list\&. Later styles override earlier ones\&. Adding a style already on the node moves nothing and is not an error\&.
.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fItree\fP The tree holding both\&. Must not be NULL\&. 
.br
\fInode\fP The node to style\&. 
.br
\fIstyle\fP A style registered with schultz_style_register\&. 
.RE
.PP
\fBReturns\fP
.RS 4
SCHULTZ_OK, SCHULTZ_ERR_INVALID_HANDLE when either is not live, or SCHULTZ_ERR_OUT_OF_MEMORY\&. 
.RE
.PP

.SS "int32_t schultz_node_remove_style (\fBschultz_tree\fP * tree, \fBschultz_handle\fP node, \fBschultz_handle\fP style)"

.PP
Removes a style from a node's list\&. 
.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fItree\fP The tree holding the node\&. Must not be NULL\&. 
.br
\fInode\fP The node to update\&. 
.br
\fIstyle\fP The style to remove\&. 
.RE
.PP
\fBReturns\fP
.RS 4
SCHULTZ_OK whether or not it was present, or SCHULTZ_ERR_INVALID_HANDLE when the node is not live\&. 
.RE
.PP

.SS "uint32_t schultz_node_style_count (const \fBschultz_tree\fP * tree, \fBschultz_handle\fP node)"

.PP
Counts the styles applied to a node\&. 
.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fItree\fP The tree holding the node\&. NULL yields 0\&. 
.br
\fInode\fP The node to query\&. 
.RE
.PP
\fBReturns\fP
.RS 4
How many styles are in its list\&. 
.RE
.PP

.SS "int32_t schultz_node_set_style_property (\fBschultz_tree\fP * tree, \fBschultz_handle\fP node, uint32_t property, \fBschultz_value\fP value)"

.PP
Sets one property directly on a node\&. This is the inline patch: styling one node without registering a style or touching the theme\&. It sits above the node's style list, so it wins\&.
.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fItree\fP The tree holding the node\&. Must not be NULL\&. 
.br
\fInode\fP The node to style\&. 
.br
\fIproperty\fP One of the SCHULTZ_PROP_* values\&. 
.br
\fIvalue\fP What to set it to, literal or a token reference\&. 
.RE
.PP
\fBReturns\fP
.RS 4
SCHULTZ_OK, SCHULTZ_ERR_INVALID_ARGUMENT for an out of range property, SCHULTZ_ERR_INVALID_HANDLE, or SCHULTZ_ERR_OUT_OF_MEMORY\&. 
.RE
.PP

.SS "int32_t schultz_node_clear_style_property (\fBschultz_tree\fP * tree, \fBschultz_handle\fP node, uint32_t property)"

.PP
Removes one inline property from a node\&. The property then resolves from the layers beneath again\&.
.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fItree\fP The tree holding the node\&. Must not be NULL\&. 
.br
\fInode\fP The node to update\&. 
.br
\fIproperty\fP The property to unset\&. 
.RE
.PP
\fBReturns\fP
.RS 4
SCHULTZ_OK, or SCHULTZ_ERR_INVALID_HANDLE\&. 
.RE
.PP

.SS "int32_t schultz_node_set_state_property (\fBschultz_tree\fP * tree, \fBschultz_handle\fP node, uint32_t state, uint32_t property, \fBschultz_value\fP value)"

.PP
Sets one property that applies only in a given interaction state\&. State patches sit above everything else, so a hover colour overrides both the node's inline style and its style list\&.
.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fItree\fP The tree holding the node\&. Must not be NULL\&. 
.br
\fInode\fP The node to style\&. 
.br
\fIstate\fP One of the SCHULTZ_STYLE_STATE_* values\&. 
.br
\fIproperty\fP One of the SCHULTZ_PROP_* values\&. 
.br
\fIvalue\fP What to set it to\&. 
.RE
.PP
\fBReturns\fP
.RS 4
SCHULTZ_OK, SCHULTZ_ERR_INVALID_ARGUMENT for an out of range state or property, SCHULTZ_ERR_INVALID_HANDLE, or SCHULTZ_ERR_OUT_OF_MEMORY\&. 
.RE
.PP

.SS "int32_t schultz_node_clear_style (\fBschultz_tree\fP * tree, \fBschultz_handle\fP node)"

.PP
Drops every style, inline property and state property from a node\&. 
.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fItree\fP The tree holding the node\&. Must not be NULL\&. 
.br
\fInode\fP The node to strip\&. 
.RE
.PP
\fBReturns\fP
.RS 4
SCHULTZ_OK, or SCHULTZ_ERR_INVALID_HANDLE\&. 
.RE
.PP

.SS "int32_t schultz_tree_set_theme (\fBschultz_tree\fP * tree, const \fBschultz_theme\fP * theme)"

.PP
Installs the theme the tree resolves tokens through\&. The theme is copied\&. Every node is marked style dirty, since any of them may resolve a token that changed\&.
.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fItree\fP The tree to update\&. Must not be NULL\&. 
.br
\fItheme\fP The theme to install, or NULL to fall back to the built in token values throughout\&. 
.RE
.PP
\fBReturns\fP
.RS 4
SCHULTZ_OK, or SCHULTZ_ERR_INVALID_ARGUMENT when tree is NULL\&. 
.RE
.PP

.SS "int32_t schultz_node_set_theme (\fBschultz_tree\fP * tree, \fBschultz_handle\fP node, const \fBschultz_theme\fP * theme)"

.PP
Gives one subtree its own theme\&. Resolution walks up to the nearest ancestor carrying a theme and falls back to the tree's\&. This is what makes a dark sidebar in a light application expressible without restating every property\&.
.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fItree\fP The tree holding the node\&. Must not be NULL\&. 
.br
\fInode\fP The node whose subtree gets the theme\&. 
.br
\fItheme\fP The theme to install, copied, or NULL to remove one\&. 
.RE
.PP
\fBReturns\fP
.RS 4
SCHULTZ_OK, SCHULTZ_ERR_INVALID_HANDLE, or SCHULTZ_ERR_OUT_OF_MEMORY\&. 
.RE
.PP

.SS "const \fBschultz_resolved_style\fP * schultz_node_resolved (const \fBschultz_tree\fP * tree, \fBschultz_handle\fP node)"

.PP
Returns a node's fully resolved style\&. This is what drawing code reads, and the only thing it should read\&. The pointer is valid until the node is destroyed; its contents change whenever the node is resolved\&.
.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fItree\fP The tree holding the node\&. Must not be NULL\&. 
.br
\fInode\fP The node to query\&. 
.RE
.PP
\fBReturns\fP
.RS 4
The resolved style, or NULL when the node is not live\&. 
.RE
.PP

.SS "int32_t schultz_tree_resolve_styles (\fBschultz_tree\fP * tree)"

.PP
Recomputes the resolved style of every node that needs it\&. Walks the tree in pre-order, resolving nodes marked style dirty and any node beneath one whose inherited properties changed\&. Comparing the old resolved style with the new one is what decides whether a node needs a relayout or only a repaint\&.
.PP
Call once per frame, before layout and paint\&.
.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fItree\fP The tree to resolve\&. Must not be NULL\&. 
.RE
.PP
\fBReturns\fP
.RS 4
SCHULTZ_OK, SCHULTZ_ERR_INVALID_ARGUMENT when tree is NULL, or SCHULTZ_ERR_OUT_OF_MEMORY\&. 
.RE
.PP

.SS "int32_t schultz_node_style_dirty (const \fBschultz_tree\fP * tree, \fBschultz_handle\fP node)"

.PP
Reports whether a node's resolved style is stale\&. 
.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fItree\fP The tree holding the node\&. NULL yields 0\&. 
.br
\fInode\fP The node to query\&. 
.RE
.PP
\fBReturns\fP
.RS 4
1 when the node must be resolved again, 0 otherwise\&. 
.RE
.PP

.SS "int32_t schultz_node_layout_dirty (const \fBschultz_tree\fP * tree, \fBschultz_handle\fP node)"

.PP
Reports whether a node, or anything under it, needs laying out\&. Set by resolution when a layout property changed, and by anything else that invalidates geometry\&. It reaches the ancestors as well as the node itself, so asking the root is how a host finds out whether laying anything out is worth the time, without walking the tree to look\&.
.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fItree\fP The tree holding the node\&. NULL yields 0\&. 
.br
\fInode\fP The node to query\&. 
.RE
.PP
\fBReturns\fP
.RS 4
1 when layout must run again somewhere at or below it, 0 otherwise\&. 
.RE
.PP

.SS "int32_t schultz_node_invalidate_layout (\fBschultz_tree\fP * tree, \fBschultz_handle\fP node)"

.PP
Marks a node as needing layout again\&. 
.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fItree\fP The tree holding the node\&. Must not be NULL\&. 
.br
\fInode\fP The node to mark\&. 
.RE
.PP
\fBReturns\fP
.RS 4
SCHULTZ_OK, or SCHULTZ_ERR_INVALID_HANDLE\&. 
.RE
.PP

.SS "void schultz_tree_clear_layout_dirty (\fBschultz_tree\fP * tree)"

.PP
Clears every layout dirty mark in the tree\&. Call after a layout pass, in the same way schultz_tree_clear_dirty is called after painting\&.
.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fItree\fP The tree to clear\&. NULL is accepted and does nothing\&. 
.RE
.PP

.SS "int32_t schultz_tree_push_overlay (\fBschultz_tree\fP * tree, \fBschultz_handle\fP node, int32_t captures)"

.PP
Registers a node as an overlay layer\&. A context menu is not a child of the thing it pops from\&. It must escape that parent's bounds and clipping, size itself to its own content, and draw above everything\&. Forcing that into the content tree means fighting it, so the root instead owns an ordered list of overlays\&. Each is its own layout root, positioned by anchor logic rather than by a parent pane\&.
.PP
The node should be a child of the root\&. Its placement is worked out in window coordinates and converted into the root's before it is stored, so a root that has been inset for a camera notch places overlays correctly rather than that much too low\&.
.PP
An overlay is kept inside the safe area, which is the root's own box: one nudged under a notch is on screen and cannot be read\&. A node that covers the window instead, such as a dialog's scrim, says so with schultz_node_set_fills_viewport and gets the whole window including the notch\&.
.PP
Overlays are searched first during hit testing, topmost down\&.
.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fItree\fP The tree to register with\&. Must not be NULL\&. 
.br
\fInode\fP The node to raise to an overlay layer\&. 
.br
\fIcaptures\fP Nonzero when a press outside this overlay should dismiss it and be consumed rather than reaching what is underneath, which is what a menu wants\&. 
.RE
.PP
\fBReturns\fP
.RS 4
SCHULTZ_OK, SCHULTZ_ERR_INVALID_HANDLE, SCHULTZ_ERR_OUT_OF_MEMORY, or SCHULTZ_ERR_EXHAUSTED when the node is already an overlay\&. 
.RE
.PP

.SS "int32_t schultz_tree_pop_overlay (\fBschultz_tree\fP * tree, \fBschultz_handle\fP * out_node)"

.PP
Removes the topmost overlay from the list\&. The node itself is untouched; destroying or hiding it is the caller's business\&.
.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fItree\fP The tree to pop from\&. Must not be NULL\&. 
.br
\fIout_node\fP Receives the removed overlay, or NULL if not wanted\&. 
.RE
.PP
\fBReturns\fP
.RS 4
SCHULTZ_OK, SCHULTZ_ERR_INVALID_ARGUMENT, or SCHULTZ_ERR_EXHAUSTED when there are no overlays\&. 
.RE
.PP

.SS "uint32_t schultz_tree_overlay_count (const \fBschultz_tree\fP * tree)"

.PP
Counts the overlay layers\&. 
.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fItree\fP The tree to query\&. NULL yields 0\&. 
.RE
.PP
\fBReturns\fP
.RS 4
How many overlays are registered\&. 
.RE
.PP

.SS "int32_t schultz_tree_overlay_at (const \fBschultz_tree\fP * tree, uint32_t index, \fBschultz_handle\fP * out_node, int32_t * out_captures)"

.PP
Returns one overlay by position\&. 
.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fItree\fP The tree to query\&. Must not be NULL\&. 
.br
\fIindex\fP Zero is the bottom overlay; the highest index is topmost\&. 
.br
\fIout_node\fP Receives the overlay node\&. Must not be NULL\&. 
.br
\fIout_captures\fP Receives whether it captures input, or NULL\&. 
.RE
.PP
\fBReturns\fP
.RS 4
SCHULTZ_OK, or SCHULTZ_ERR_INVALID_ARGUMENT when index is out of range\&. 
.RE
.PP

.SS "int32_t schultz_node_set_token (\fBschultz_tree\fP * tree, \fBschultz_handle\fP node, uint64_t token)"

.PP
Attaches the host's opaque token to a node\&. The toolkit never interprets this\&. It is handed back with every event aimed at the node, and the host uses it to find whatever listener it registered\&. Storing a token rather than a callback is what keeps the toolkit from having to trace host memory\&.
.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fItree\fP The tree holding the node\&. Must not be NULL\&. 
.br
\fInode\fP The node to tag\&. 
.br
\fItoken\fP Any value meaningful to the host\&. Zero means no listener\&. 
.RE
.PP
\fBReturns\fP
.RS 4
SCHULTZ_OK, or SCHULTZ_ERR_INVALID_HANDLE\&. 
.RE
.PP

.SS "uint64_t schultz_node_get_token (const \fBschultz_tree\fP * tree, \fBschultz_handle\fP node)"

.PP
Reads a node's host token\&. 
.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fItree\fP The tree holding the node\&. NULL yields 0\&. 
.br
\fInode\fP The node to query\&. 
.RE
.PP
\fBReturns\fP
.RS 4
The token, or 0 when none is set\&. 
.RE
.PP

.SS "int32_t schultz_node_set_pane (\fBschultz_tree\fP * tree, \fBschultz_handle\fP node, const \fBschultz_pane_vtable\fP * vtable)"

.PP
Gives a node a pane, which decides how its children are placed\&. A node with no pane is a leaf: it measures to its preferred size hints and places no children\&.
.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fItree\fP The tree holding the node\&. Must not be NULL\&. 
.br
\fInode\fP The node to configure\&. 
.br
\fIvtable\fP The pane implementation, or NULL to make the node a leaf\&. The vtable must outlive the node; the built in panes have static storage duration\&. 
.RE
.PP
\fBReturns\fP
.RS 4
SCHULTZ_OK, or SCHULTZ_ERR_INVALID_HANDLE\&. 
.RE
.PP

.SS "const \fBschultz_pane_vtable\fP * schultz_node_get_pane (const \fBschultz_tree\fP * tree, \fBschultz_handle\fP node)"

.PP
Returns the pane a node uses\&. 
.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fItree\fP The tree holding the node\&. NULL yields NULL\&. 
.br
\fInode\fP The node to query\&. 
.RE
.PP
\fBReturns\fP
.RS 4
The pane vtable, or NULL when the node is a leaf\&. 
.RE
.PP

.SS "int32_t schultz_node_set_pref_size (\fBschultz_tree\fP * tree, \fBschultz_handle\fP node, float width, float height)"

.PP
Sets how big a node would like to be\&. A wish, not a rule\&. It says what to be when nothing else decides, and a pane is entitled to decide otherwise: a box pane stretches its children across the cross axis by default, which is why a row of buttons comes out the same height\&. For a size that holds whatever the pane thinks, say so with schultz_node_set_min_size and schultz_node_set_max_size\&.
.PP
Both axes are set\&. SCHULTZ_SIZE_UNSET on either leaves that one to be measured from the content, which is what a wrapped label wants: a width to wrap inside, and whatever height that turns out to need\&.
.PP
Marks the node dirty, since a size change can alter what is painted\&.
.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fItree\fP The tree holding the node\&. Must not be NULL\&. 
.br
\fInode\fP The node to size\&. 
